To prevent the divs from shifting when the song name changes in length, you can set a fixed width for the song name div.
This way, no matter the length of the song name, it will always occupy the same amount of space, and the other elements
won't move around.

Here's how you can achieve that with flexbox:

<div class="song-container">
  <div class="song-name">Song Name</div>
  <div class="controls">
    <button class="previous">Previous</button>
    <button class="play">Play</button>
    <button class="next">Next</button>
  </div>
  <div class="song-duration">3:45</div>
</div>

And the corresponding CSS:

.song-container {
display: flex;
justify-content: space-between;
align-items: center;
}

.song-name {
flex: 1; /* allows it to take up available space */
min-width: 150px; /* ensures a minimum width */
max-width: 300px; /* can limit maximum width if needed */
overflow: hidden;
text-overflow: ellipsis; /* hides the text that doesn't fit and adds "..." */
white-space: nowrap; /* prevents the text from wrapping to a new line */
}

.controls {
display: flex;
gap: 10px;
}

.song-duration {
min-width: 50px;
text-align: right;
}

Explanation:

min-width and max-width: By setting these properties for the .song-name div, you prevent the div from shrinking too much
or expanding too much based on the song name length.
text-overflow: ellipsis: If the song name is too long, this will truncate it and add "..." at the end instead of
breaking the layout.
white-space: nowrap: Ensures that the song name stays on one line and doesn't wrap to multiple lines, which could cause
the layout to shift.

This way, your layout will stay consistent regardless of the song name length!